1	//! Custom axum extractors that close pitfalls in axum's defaults.
2	//!
3	//! ## Why
4	//!
5	//! axum's stock `Option<Json<T>>` extractor has a behaviour that is
6	//! easy to overlook: if the request body is present but malformed
7	//! (invalid JSON, hits the deserializer's recursion limit, exceeds
8	//! the body limit, etc.) the extractor **silently returns `None`**
9	//! instead of producing a `JsonRejection`. The handler then sees
10	//! `body.is_none()` and typically falls back to defaults.
11	//!
12	//! The 2026-04-25 OSS code review verified this empirically: a
13	//! 70-level deep nested-bool POST against `/idx/_search` arrived at
14	//! `parse_request` as `{"query": {"match_all": {}}}` — the deep query
15	//! had been silently replaced with the default. The user got back
16	//! 200 OK with empty hits, never knowing the query they sent had
17	//! been thrown away.
18	//!
19	//! `OptionalJson<T>` distinguishes the three real cases:
20	//!
21	//! | Case               | Behaviour                            |
22	//! |--------------------|--------------------------------------|
23	//! | Body absent/empty  | `Ok(OptionalJson(None))`             |
24	//! | Body parses to `T` | `Ok(OptionalJson(Some(T)))`          |
25	//! | Body is malformed  | `Err(JsonRejection)` → 400 response  |
26	//!
27	//! Use this on every POST/PUT handler that accepts a body but
28	//! tolerates an absent one (e.g. `/_search` works with no body —
29	//! defaults to match-all). For mandatory-body endpoints, use
30	//! `Json<T>` directly.
31	//!
32	//! ## Migration
33	//!
34	//! ```ignore
35	//! // Before:
36	//! pub async fn handler(body: Option<Json<EsSearchBody>>) {
37	//!     let body = body.map(|j| j.0).unwrap_or_default();
38	//! }
39	//!
40	//! // After:
41	//! use crate::extract::OptionalJson;
42	//! pub async fn handler(body: OptionalJson<EsSearchBody>) {
43	//!     let body = body.0.unwrap_or_default();
44	//! }
45	//! ```
